Ask for a new IMS context when the granted one will not register

r52 made the provisioning ladder able to retry past its burst, and it worked:
Telus got a PDN at T+3.5min that the old dead end would have prevented. The
context it got was useless -- granted with a P-CSCF set the network does not
answer on, so SIP registration never happened and nothing noticed.

Across eight captures on this device, one pool has registered every time it
appeared and one has never registered once:

    2001:568:fff2:300::a   r49        REGISTERED
    2001:568:fff2:300::a   CSFB boot  REGISTERED
    2001:568:ffff:3002::8  r43        no SIP
    2001:568:ffff:3002::9  r52        no SIP

Which set arrives is the network's choice per establishment. Nothing AP-side
picks it, and hardcoding a pool would be correct on this SIM in this country
and wrong everywhere else.

What is portable is the observation, not the address: the stack is provisioned,
and still not registered long after the CP would have been -- it registers
within about 15 s when it is going to at all (measured 14 s and 17 s on the
boots that worked). So after REGISTRATION_WATCHDOG_SECS the context is
discarded and asked for again, and a fresh establishment draws a fresh set. The
condition is "did SIP registration happen", which is true or false on any
carrier in any country, rather than "which addresses came back".

Before this, nothing watched that at all: reaching IMS_STATE_STARTED reset the
failure count, so a provisioned-but-unregistered stack was indistinguishable
from a working one and sat there holding a dead PDN for the life of the boot.

Re-provisioning goes through mm_sit_ims_stop(), so the monotonic floor still
decides how often it may happen -- the guard that keeps a retry loop from
flooding the CP's carrier-policy reload.

